- [Rhett] Wacky.

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:43.730
mythical.com.

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:46.440
Get yourself some mug action.

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:50.280
- Okay, I mean, since
one comes before three,

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:51.570
just go ahead and uh.

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:52.430
- All right, I'll kick it off

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:53.630
but we'll bounce back and forth.

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:54.463
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:16:54.463 --> 00:16:57.869
- I, it definitely was obvious

00:16:57.869 --> 00:17:01.360
that I was a one in my mind very quickly.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:04.210
I mean, even without
knowing about the Enneagram,

00:17:04.210 --> 00:17:08.143
my perfectionistic tendencies, you know,

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:11.010
maybe it's because I'm a one

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:15.193
or that I identified with it immediately,

00:17:16.135 --> 00:17:19.370
or maybe it's just like the concept

00:17:19.370 --> 00:17:21.150
of being a perfectionist is something

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:23.620
that people talk about
more often than some

00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:25.330
of these other types.

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:26.200
Maybe.

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:28.100
I don't know, I can't
tell because I'm kind

00:17:28.100 --> 00:17:29.710
of on the inside of it.

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:32.570
But people tend to think
they can spot a perfectionist

00:17:32.570 --> 00:17:37.440
because, you know, it's
a familiar term, right?

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:40.770
So I kind of always consider
myself a perfectionist.

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:42.523
Very meticulous.

00:17:43.649 --> 00:17:44.980
So as I started to read about it,

00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:46.660
I was like, oh yeah,

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:48.290
I didn't play with my toys.

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:50.930
I posed my toys in the
way that I wanted them

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:52.990
and I never touched them again.

00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:53.954
- Right.

00:17:53.954 --> 00:17:54.817
- You know?

00:17:54.817 --> 00:17:58.390
I look over my family's shoulder

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:01.540
when they're loading the dishwasher

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:06.540
and I just can't help but
reload it once they're done.

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:09.930
Basically with any type of procedure,

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:13.540
I always think I know
the best way to do it

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:17.320
and I just can't help myself
to get my hands in there

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:19.310
and to rearrange it.

00:18:19.310 --> 00:18:21.140
Gotta make things right.

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:22.150
- Right.

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:24.257
- I mean, that's a big part of it.

00:18:24.257 --> 00:18:25.840
It goes a lot deeper than that but I mean,

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:27.920
speaking of going deeper,

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:31.660
I guess we can talk about the
childhood message of it all?

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:32.560
- Sure, yeah.

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:36.730
- Is there, so in "The
Complete Enneagram,"

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:39.990
it's more of the reference
book that we both read.

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:40.823
- Well, I think that's.

00:18:40.823 --> 00:18:42.410
- "27 Paths to Greater Self-Knowledge."

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:43.243
That's the one that I read.

00:18:43.243 --> 00:18:44.076
I didn't read the other one.

00:18:44.076 --> 00:18:46.770
- Well, the unconscious childhood message

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:48.720
that the one that I just told you about,

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:50.434
I know is in "The Wisdom
of the Enneagram,"

00:18:50.434 --> 00:18:51.267
'Cause that's what I was.

00:18:51.267 --> 00:18:52.470
It could also be in "The
Complete Enneagram."

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:57.180
- Yeah, it says, "Chaos
or uncertainty or anxiety

00:18:57.180 --> 00:19:00.250
in the family motivates the one child

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:02.520
to take on the task of providing order

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:05.350
and structure for himself.

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:07.390
The coping strategy of adhering strongly

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:09.880
to sound rules, and routines,

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:12.550
and standards of behavior provides a one

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:15.680
with inner cohesion and security

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:18.210
as well as protection from criticism."

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:22.530
So, you know, it's not that my mom,

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:25.650
my mom didn't have a
lot of rules actually.

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:29.120
I, things were, things were pretty loose

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:32.280
but I definitely had
this sense that I needed

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:35.450
to be a good child and that I needed

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:39.906
to follow the rules even,

00:19:39.906 --> 00:19:42.363
and I would make them up for myself.

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:46.230
So I can definitely see, I mean,

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:49.260
some people will assert
that you're kind of born

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:53.230
with a number and that it
just kind of manifests itself

00:19:53.230 --> 00:19:56.470
through the message that
you received as a child,

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:58.510
and it's like the first litmus test

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:00.240
of kind of what your number is.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:01.680
Other people may say different things.

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:06.680
But for me, I think there
was this perceived insecurity

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:10.973
and that some of it was,

00:20:11.830 --> 00:20:16.830
it was up to me to make
sure that we were good.

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:17.910
You know, it was like,

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:21.420
it was me and my mom.

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:23.650
You know, I remember
things like it just being,

00:20:23.650 --> 00:20:25.230
okay, my mom's getting a divorce

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:30.230
and now it's just me and my
mom in like late grade school,

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:33.410
and just kind of internalizing the fact

00:20:33.410 --> 00:20:38.410
that I need to do my
part to walk the line.

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:41.250
I don't, you know, I don't
want to cause any trouble.

00:20:41.250 --> 00:20:46.250
I don't want to be the one to
create any sort of split here.

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:49.160
I don't, nothing was to
the point where like,

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:52.140
I mean I'm certainly continuing
to explore those things

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:55.870
from my childhood in
therapy and in my own work.

00:20:55.870 --> 00:21:00.870
But I wouldn't say that it
was traumatic to the point.

00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:04.750
I mean, I just think that
that's how I responded to it.

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:05.844
- Right.

00:21:05.844 --> 00:21:06.720
- With, I can order my zone.

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:10.330
I can stay in, my room can be my place.

00:21:10.330 --> 00:21:14.380
And if there's, if anybody
has expectations of me,

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:18.200
I need to do everything
I can to follow those.

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:21.660
So it does resonate in that way.

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:26.160
I think the the childhood
message is it's not okay

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:27.846
to make mistakes.

00:21:27.846 --> 00:21:28.821
- Right.

00:21:28.821 --> 00:21:31.350
- And again, I think that
was not anything that was,

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:35.970
I didn't have a drill
sergeant of family members

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:37.440
that were saying, you know, and I didn't,

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:39.160
I'd never experienced a lot of punishment.

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:40.570
It was all something that I put on myself

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:42.880
and that I definitely believed

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:46.480
as a defense mechanism
of things going wrong.

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:47.700
If anything goes wrong,

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:50.620
it can't be because I
didn't do something right

00:21:50.620 --> 00:21:52.480
if I knew it, if I'm culpable.

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:56.693
- And just to give people
an idea of how personal,

00:21:58.370 --> 00:22:00.691
how much you take it upon yourself,

00:22:00.691 --> 00:22:05.000
it's not just the the feeling
that you can't make mistakes.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:08.730
I mean, like the basic
fear of a one according

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:13.170
to the Enneagram Institute is
of being evil or defective.

00:22:13.170 --> 00:22:14.628
You know what I'm saying?

00:22:14.628 --> 00:22:15.461
So it's not about, like,
if I make a mistake,

00:22:15.461 --> 00:22:17.250
it's going to screw things up.

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:18.932
It's about you.

00:22:18.932 --> 00:22:20.673
- The core of my being is.

00:22:20.673 --> 00:22:21.844
- If I do something wrong, I'm bad.

00:22:21.844 --> 00:22:24.580
- I am a, yeah, it's not
just I did something wrong.

00:22:24.580 --> 00:22:25.413
I am wrong.

00:22:25.413 --> 00:22:28.210
And, you know, in the,

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:31.630
in the evangelical environment
that we grew up in,

00:22:31.630 --> 00:22:33.560
you know that is a tenant of the faith,

00:22:33.560 --> 00:22:37.040
is that you're born into sin.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:42.040
So you're born guilty and
in need of punishment.

00:22:45.790 --> 00:22:47.380
You deserve punishment.

00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:51.000
You know, just by virtue
of being born and existing.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:54.423
I never questioned that.

00:22:56.020 --> 00:22:57.273
I accepted that.

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:04.270
Maybe it resonated with me
and I internalized that.

00:23:04.474 --> 00:23:06.382
So that was a defining belief

00:23:06.382 --> 00:23:09.270
and a defining part of
my experiences as well.

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:10.880
- Well, it's all, you know,

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:15.490
the different environments
that you find yourself in will,

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:17.170
might exacerbate it, right?

00:23:17.170 --> 00:23:18.500
I think interestingly,

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:20.714
I mean one of the things
we didn't say at the top,

00:23:20.714 --> 00:23:24.070
is there is definitely a spiritual aspect

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:25.860
to the roots of the Enneagram.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:27.710
So it actually, the Enneagram is talked

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:30.298
about quite a bit in
Christian circles, right?

00:23:30.298 --> 00:23:34.120
Both evangelical circles but
also sort of post-Christian

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:34.960
or liberal Christian,

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:36.460
progressive Christian circles.

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:39.070
You don't have to have any sort

00:23:39.070 --> 00:23:41.610
of spiritual identity or you know,

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:43.160
that doesn't have to be a part
of your background in order

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:44.520
to appreciate light Link's talking about

00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:46.450
the practical side of things.

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:51.420
But when you're in that
environment where you're being told

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:55.146
that you're bad and that
is your ultimate problem,

00:23:55.146 --> 00:23:59.280
I think that probably
speaks to a lot of the ways

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:02.350
that we processed our
whole spiritual experience

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:03.630
so differently.

00:24:03.630 --> 00:24:04.463
You know what I'm saying?

00:24:04.463 --> 00:24:05.609
Which we'll get into how a three

00:24:05.609 --> 00:24:07.673
navigates the spiritual space

00:24:07.673 --> 00:24:09.390
when we talk about me in a second.

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:10.880
- Yeah, for me it was okay,

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:15.880
my, everything that I learned
in church growing up was okay,

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:19.120
a set of rules and a set of beliefs,

00:24:19.120 --> 00:24:22.500
and an entire worldview
that's completely organized

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:23.760
that I was like, okay.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:27.280
It gave me a sense of
comfort that existed.

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:32.026
All I had to do was live
up to it and you know.

00:24:32.026 --> 00:24:32.859
- That's tough.

00:24:32.859 --> 00:24:34.630
- Follow through with the action.

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:35.463
Oh yeah.

00:24:35.463 --> 00:24:38.120
And it, and that's not, you know,

00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:40.480
that's not ultimately the point of it

00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:43.480
but that was certainly what I fixated on.

00:24:43.480 --> 00:24:44.313
- Right.

00:24:44.313 --> 00:24:45.146
- You know?

00:24:45.146 --> 00:24:47.386
It wasn't a connection and
a relationship with God.

00:24:47.386 --> 00:24:52.386
If you replay my spiritual
deconstruction episodes

00:24:52.780 --> 00:24:55.120
through this lens of being a one,

00:24:55.120 --> 00:24:59.310
it definitely makes sense and
it's helped me process it.

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:01.611
Another part of it is, like I said,

00:25:01.611 --> 00:25:05.420
protection from criticism.

00:25:05.420 --> 00:25:06.840
Like trying to do everything you can

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:11.020
so that someone else can't
point out your fault.

00:25:11.020 --> 00:25:13.770
You know, I think about
when I was dating Christy

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:18.570
and I was just,

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:19.790
I was reading all these books.

00:25:19.790 --> 00:25:21.690
I wanted to do it right.

00:25:21.690 --> 00:25:25.820
You know, my dad, I
never lived with my dad.

00:25:25.820 --> 00:25:27.260
You know, he left when I was two

00:25:27.260 --> 00:25:29.290
and then my mom got remarried,

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:30.980
and then there was a divorce.

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:33.410
I don't think I ever blamed myself

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:35.440
for either one of those things,

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:39.783
but how I internalized it
is a different question.

00:25:40.986 --> 00:25:45.030
I could definitely see
when I was dating Christy,

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:48.240
I was like, I don't, I wanna
make sure I do it right.

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:49.970
I want to read all the books

00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:54.460
so that no stone is
left unturned with what,

00:25:54.460 --> 00:25:56.190
with this decision I'm going to make.

00:25:56.190 --> 00:25:58.740
Should I ask her to marry me?

00:25:58.740 --> 00:25:59.680
- And I remember,

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:01.350
I remember this summer where you're trying

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:02.820
to figure this out

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:06.740
and you were like asking
all these questions,

00:26:06.740 --> 00:26:08.970
and you were like
weighing all these things.

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:10.700
You were meeting with other people.

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:13.750
- There was like pro con
lists that I would make.

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:17.371
And it was as if someone was
looking over my shoulder,

00:26:17.371 --> 00:26:20.150
analyzing my choice so that,

00:26:20.150 --> 00:26:23.500
so that if they said
why are you doing this?

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:25.020
I would have all the reasons.

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:26.890
- Yeah, and not having any,

00:26:26.890 --> 00:26:28.190
I mean, we, weren't very in touch

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:30.730
with who we were at all in college.

00:26:30.730 --> 00:26:34.020
But, you know, I just interpreted it as

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:36.000
Link's being weird about this decision,

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:36.860
which is as simple as that.

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:39.620
'Cause I specifically
remember just telling you,

00:26:39.620 --> 00:26:42.550
I was just like, do you want to marry her?

00:26:42.550 --> 00:26:43.383
- Right.

00:26:43.383 --> 00:26:45.110
- If answer is yes then
just ask her to marry you.

00:26:45.110 --> 00:26:46.533
It was, for me, it was,

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:49.612
I tried to simplify it to that level

00:26:49.612 --> 00:26:51.570
and then you were like,
no, no, no, no, no.

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:52.960
It's not that,

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:54.680
how could you just make
this decision based

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:55.650
on what you want?

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:59.990
- My mom always tells the story of when I,

00:26:59.990 --> 00:27:01.940
we went out to dinner
and I was like, "Mom."

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:02.773
I looked at her eye and I was like,

00:27:02.773 --> 00:27:06.490
"Mom, I think I love her."

00:27:06.490 --> 00:27:08.660
And she was like, "Ya think?"

00:27:08.660 --> 00:27:12.360
It's, of course you do.

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:14.370
You know, it's like, finally
you're figuring this out

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:15.840
and poor Christy,

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:17.180
it's like she had to wait for me

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:22.050
to like justify the fact
that I loved her to some,

00:27:23.320 --> 00:27:25.010
and it wasn't to God,

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:29.750
it was to like any
outside criticism so that,

00:27:29.750 --> 00:27:32.750
you know, again, I just didn't
want to make any mistakes.

00:27:32.750 --> 00:27:36.830
And as a one you don't, you don't want to,

00:27:36.830 --> 00:27:38.780
you know, having to defend, having to say,

00:27:38.780 --> 00:27:42.123
yeah, I, you know, I didn't do this right.

00:27:43.010 --> 00:27:45.740
I didn't look look at all the information.

00:27:45.740 --> 00:27:47.633
I didn't do everything I could have.

00:27:48.730 --> 00:27:50.583
It was, you know, it was,

00:27:51.490 --> 00:27:53.850
I was kind of trapped and I robbed myself

00:27:53.850 --> 00:27:55.470
of a lot of the experience

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:58.650
of experiencing falling
in love with Christy

00:27:58.650 --> 00:28:01.220
and being in love,

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:05.970
and I would apologetically
explain love as something

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:08.160
that wasn't a concrete reason.

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:10.068
- You were trying to
break it down to something

00:28:10.068 --> 00:28:13.360
that was even more
pragmatic than it can be.

00:28:13.360 --> 00:28:18.270
- I mean, for what's the
childhood message for the three?

00:28:18.270 --> 00:28:20.436
- Or where, start wherever you want to.

00:28:20.436 --> 00:28:24.884
I don't have the perfect
episode in my mind

00:28:24.884 --> 00:28:26.770
that I want you to adhere to.

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:27.770
- You're really resisting.

00:28:27.770 --> 00:28:29.113
You're really resisting.

00:28:30.053 --> 00:28:31.070
- Oh yeah.

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:36.070
- So here's one way that
I knew I was a three.

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:40.097
The day that Jessie told
me about the Enneagram

00:28:40.097 --> 00:28:44.543
and she said there are
nine personality numbers.

00:28:45.876 --> 00:28:47.429
I was like, which one's the best one?

00:28:47.429 --> 00:28:49.349
(laughing)

00:28:49.349 --> 00:28:51.330
I was joking.

00:28:51.330 --> 00:28:53.580
But honestly,

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:58.270
that's how a three approaches
every single situation,

00:28:58.270 --> 00:29:03.270
is how can I be the best
in this environment?

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:06.160
In this, with this challenge, right?

00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:10.310
So again, little description
of the threes again.

00:29:10.310 --> 00:29:11.830
The achiever, the performer, the winner.

00:29:11.830 --> 00:29:15.330
Threes are self-assured, attractive.

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:17.330
Now the reason that it
says attractive is not

00:29:17.330 --> 00:29:19.820
because you're naturally good looking.

00:29:19.820 --> 00:29:21.280
It's that you're image conscious

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:25.140
so no matter how ugly you actually are,

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:28.780
even if you don't have a chin, right?

00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:31.480
You can grow a beard over
that chin and be like,

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:33.310
that guy is not bad lookin'.

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:34.620
Don't shave.

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:35.453
You see what I'm saying?

00:29:35.453 --> 00:29:37.080
That's what a three can do.

00:29:37.080 --> 00:29:39.940
Charming, ambitious, competent, energetic.

00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:42.260
They can also be status conscious

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:43.920
and highly driven for advancement.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:45.460
They're diplomatic and poised

00:29:45.460 --> 00:29:47.020
but can also be overly concerned

00:29:47.020 --> 00:29:49.400
with their image and what
others think of them.

00:29:49.400 --> 00:29:50.610
They typically have problems

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:53.110
with workaholism and competitiveness.

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:53.943
Who's that?

00:29:53.943 --> 00:29:54.776
What?

00:29:54.776 --> 00:29:57.130
At their best, self-accepting, authentic,

00:29:57.130 --> 00:29:58.130
everything they seem to be,

00:29:58.130 --> 00:30:00.960
role models who inspire others.

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:04.051
So the childhood message,

00:30:04.051 --> 00:30:05.820
and this is this is interesting, right?

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:07.110
So on the surface,

00:30:07.110 --> 00:30:09.360
it's something that I think
many of you can relate to,

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:13.230
and that is most parents,

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:14.380
and I'm one of them,

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:19.380
make the mistake of sort of
giving praise, and rewards,

00:30:20.650 --> 00:30:23.320
and love to your children
when they do things well.

00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:24.400
They make good grades,

00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:26.730
they perform well in sports,

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:27.770
and some kids,

00:30:27.770 --> 00:30:29.090
kids who are threes,

00:30:29.090 --> 00:30:33.150
internalize that message
to this hyper-drive level

00:30:33.150 --> 00:30:37.840
where they actually begin to
attach their own self-worth

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:40.810
to having pleased their parents.

00:30:40.810 --> 00:30:43.280
So this doesn't mean that my
parents did something wrong

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:46.640
in telling me that it was
good to make good grades

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:48.010
or that being good at basketball

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:49.980
was something they were into.

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:54.260
It's that the three takes
that and builds an identity

00:30:54.260 --> 00:30:59.223
that is based on what someone else wants.

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:04.370
So they call the three
the chameleon in a lot of,

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:09.370
because what they can do is
you put us into a situation.

00:31:09.410 --> 00:31:11.090
Put us on a basketball team.

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:12.760
We're going to figure out
how to be the best player

00:31:12.760 --> 00:31:13.670
on the basketball team.

00:31:13.670 --> 00:31:14.830
Put us into a classroom.

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:15.740
We're going to figure out how

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:18.020
to be the smartest person in the classroom

00:31:18.020 --> 00:31:19.370
and if we can't be the smartest,

00:31:19.370 --> 00:31:23.050
we're going to be the best
person who is giving a speech.

00:31:23.050 --> 00:31:25.140
We're going to find
something that we can do

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:26.560
and if we can't do it,

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:28.570
if we start thinking why I
actually can't cut it here,

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:32.380
we bail and we go to all the
other things that we can do

00:31:32.380 --> 00:31:35.040
and we learn how to
navigate and be good at it.

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:39.530
I see this pattern repeated
so many times in my life.

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:44.530
Academically, with
sports, with our career.

00:31:45.590 --> 00:31:46.423
You know,

00:31:47.610 --> 00:31:51.033
they're the ultimate fake
it 'til you make it, right?

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:55.130
Threes will go into a situation knowing

00:31:55.130 --> 00:31:58.280
that they do not know how to execute

00:31:58.280 --> 00:31:59.680
or be good at something.

00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:01.302
But with this belief,

00:32:01.302 --> 00:32:03.200
I'll figure it out.

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:04.810
I'll get into the situation.

00:32:04.810 --> 00:32:06.140
I will adapt.

00:32:06.140 --> 00:32:08.900
I will learn and I will come out on top.

00:32:08.900 --> 00:32:11.830
There's just this pathological belief

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:13.710
that that's how you can
approach everything.

00:32:13.710 --> 00:32:14.543
Now.

00:32:14.543 --> 00:32:15.610
- Bullshit artist.

00:32:15.610 --> 00:32:17.980
- Well, we can be very
deceptive and we can be,

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:19.070
a lot of politicians.

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:20.693
A lot of politicians are threes.

00:32:21.530 --> 00:32:23.160
- Bill Clinton.

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:25.254
I got a list here, yeah.

00:32:25.254 --> 00:32:26.822
- But.

00:32:26.822 --> 00:32:27.930
- Michael Jordan

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:29.031
- Now.

00:32:29.031 --> 00:32:29.864
- Is he a politician?

00:32:29.864 --> 00:32:33.090
- I mean, a lot of people
who have succeeded in like,

00:32:33.090 --> 00:32:34.080
Tiger Woods is a three.

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:36.960
A lot of people who have succeeded sort

00:32:36.960 --> 00:32:39.310
of exceptionally in a particular field

00:32:39.310 --> 00:32:41.530
to a maniacal level happen to be threes.

00:32:41.530 --> 00:32:42.363
- Oprah.

00:32:42.363 --> 00:32:43.196
- But Oprah!

00:32:43.196 --> 00:32:45.610
But what are we running from?

00:32:45.610 --> 00:32:46.750
Why?

00:32:46.750 --> 00:32:47.690
Why are we like this?

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:48.840
Why am I like this?

00:32:48.840 --> 00:32:50.513
So, you know,

00:32:52.050 --> 00:32:55.413
I think this is the thing
that has been so impactful

00:32:57.980 --> 00:33:00.940
and the beginning of
transformative as it comes

00:33:00.940 --> 00:33:03.190
to like my therapy and
exploring these ideas,

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:09.060
is that actually our basic
fear is of being worthless.

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:12.160
The unconscious childhood message

00:33:12.160 --> 00:33:13.540
that we received was,

00:33:13.540 --> 00:33:16.720
not that we received but
that we latched on to.

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:17.630
Again, I want to make sure

00:33:17.630 --> 00:33:19.270
that this isn't coming across as like.

00:33:19.270 --> 00:33:20.103
- Blaming your parents?

00:33:20.103 --> 00:33:21.030
- Your parents did something.

00:33:21.030 --> 00:33:25.096
This is what we latched onto
because of our disposition.

00:33:25.096 --> 00:33:29.580
The message that I received
and believed was it's not okay

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:32.240
to have your own feelings and identity.

00:33:32.240 --> 00:33:35.363
Now there's a lot of reasons
why that might be the case.

00:33:36.510 --> 00:33:41.410
But what you end up doing is
to not have your own identity.

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:46.410
What that means is that threes
have a really difficult time

00:33:46.970 --> 00:33:49.000
figuring out what they want

00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:51.120
because what they're really
good at is figuring out

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:55.142
what you want, what the
environment that they want.

00:33:55.142 --> 00:33:57.483
Think about campus ministry, right?

00:33:58.560 --> 00:34:00.780
I have had a tendency
throughout my entire life,

00:34:00.780 --> 00:34:02.610
when you put me into an environment,

00:34:02.610 --> 00:34:05.950
I find a way to navigate
to the top of it, right?

00:34:05.950 --> 00:34:08.145
So what did I do in campus ministry?

00:34:08.145 --> 00:34:11.850
We went to one campus crusade event

00:34:11.850 --> 00:34:14.780
and I saw that there was
a guy out there talking.

00:34:14.780 --> 00:34:16.910
Every week he was introducing people.

00:34:16.910 --> 00:34:17.780
He was making jokes.

00:34:17.780 --> 00:34:19.300
He was in videos.

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:21.800
And it just clicked and a
wasn't a conscious process.

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:23.863
It was like I'm going to be that person.

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:30.770
Because I can't navigate
a space in any other way.

00:34:30.770 --> 00:34:33.893
So the next year I was that person, right?

00:34:35.240 --> 00:34:39.436
And so, but what are we,
what are we running from?

00:34:39.436 --> 00:34:44.150
We believe that the moment that
we stop asserting ourselves

00:34:44.150 --> 00:34:46.960
and the moment that we
stop being this thing

00:34:46.960 --> 00:34:48.560
that other people have found value in,

00:34:48.560 --> 00:34:50.110
that we don't have any value.

00:34:50.110 --> 00:34:51.280
That we are a worthless.

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:53.390
So it's actually this tricky thing

00:34:53.390 --> 00:34:54.350
where it's not this thing.

00:34:54.350 --> 00:34:56.950
We don't actually think that we're great.

00:34:56.950 --> 00:34:59.610
We actually think that we're not great.

00:34:59.610 --> 00:35:04.380
We think that we are ultimately worthless

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:06.650
unless we can do something for you,

00:35:06.650 --> 00:35:08.730
unless we can perform in a way

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:12.310
that you're like good job, great job.

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:13.360
And if we don't get that,

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:16.530
we begin to disappear.

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:18.140
I mean the worst fear

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:21.720
that I have is like being incapacitated.

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:24.395
Like literally like, oh,
what if I'm in an accident

00:35:24.395 --> 00:35:27.651
and I can't do what I do anymore?

00:35:27.651 --> 00:35:30.120
Like, that is what a three is scared of.

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:32.214
Because the moment that people have to,

00:35:32.214 --> 00:35:33.047
and this is what,

00:35:33.047 --> 00:35:35.000
we also tend to be incredibly independent.

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:36.610
I don't want you doing something for me.

00:35:36.610 --> 00:35:38.990
Don't help with something
because that means that I'm,

00:35:38.990 --> 00:35:42.770
that I can't do it and
I might be worthless.

00:35:42.770 --> 00:35:45.640
So receiving things from people,

00:35:45.640 --> 00:35:47.409
receiving help from people.

00:35:47.409 --> 00:35:52.000
We're not good at that
because we want to do it.

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:54.980
Again, not because we think,
it comes across as arrogance.

